anthropic: keep every parallel server tool call in the Responses stream conversion

When Claude calls server tools in parallel, Anthropic streams all
server_tool_use blocks first (inputs via input_json_delta) and then the
*_tool_result blocks, each paired by tool_use_id. Programmatic tool calling
likewise nests web_search calls inside a code_execution call. The Anthropic
-> Responses stream state held a single in-flight call per tool (web_search,
web_fetch, advisor, tool_search, code_execution): every new server_tool_use
overwrote it, results for any other tool_use_id were dropped, and a call was
completed when any block stopped while a result was pending. Only the last
call of a batch kept its input and result; the others reached Anthropic
clients with an empty input and no result block, and on the Claude Code
passthrough path the converter's block numbering fell behind the raw
upstream frames (deltas for a block index the client never opened).

Track server tool calls per server_tool_use id: each call's input comes from
its own block's input_json_delta buffer, a result attaches to the call its
tool_use_id names, and the result block's own content_block_stop completes
only that call.

Two gaps in the reverse (Responses -> Anthropic) stream that dropped call
inputs even for a single call are fixed as well:
- web_fetch: a url that arrived as input_json_delta (block opened with {})
  is now delivered before the server_tool_use stop, as web_search does.
- Anthropic tool_search: the stream emitted a content_block_start without a
  content block. It now opens the server_tool_use, delivers the query, and
  emits the paired tool_search_tool_result; the passthrough path converts
  its output_item.done like the other server tools.

Deferred web searches (#9591) are tracked the same way: every search still
open at message_delta/message_stop is closed as a pending call, in
output_index order, and a web_search_tool_result whose tool_use_id names no
call of this message opens a result-only call, so a late result is never
confused with the result of a parallel search opened in the same message.

Streams whose calls arrive sequentially produce the same events as before.

